<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pepin the Short</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">crowned <a href="/years/751">751 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pepin the Short was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a> and the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>. As mayor of the palace he deposed the last Merovingian king and was crowned King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> in 751 CE with papal approval. His alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> founded the Carolingian dynasty and shaped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short.jpg" alt="Pepin the Short" width="256" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: from the Middle Ages, unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PippinImperialChronicleCorpusChristiCollegeMS373Fol14.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…— whose victory near Tours and Poitiers in 732 CE checked an Umayyad raid into Gaul — and <b>Pepin the Short</b> transformed the Carolingians from palace mayors into the effective rulers of the Frankish kingdom, before Pepin deposed the last Merovingian king, Childeric III, and was made king…</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Merovingian gold coinage gave way to the silver penny, or denier, which <b>Pippin the Short</b> and Charlemagne standardised in a system of 240 pennies to the pound.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>When King Aistulf seized Ravenna in 751 and threatened Rome, Pope Stephen II crossed the Alps and in 754 met the Frankish king <b>Pepin the Short</b>, whom he anointed at Saint-Denis.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>They were long a match for the Franks: in the 550s they inflicted a heavy defeat on Chlothar I, and in the 8th century they resisted repeated campaigns by Charles Martel and <b>Pippin the Short</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saxons">Saxons · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Frankish kingdom was at least partly elective: <b>Pepin the Short</b> was elected king of the Franks in 751 with papal approval, and the West Frankish nobles chose between rival lineages until they elected Hugh Capet in 987.</blockquote><a href="/government/elective-monarchy">Elective monarchy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In Italy the popes became temporal rulers when <b>Pepin the Short</b> granted them former Byzantine lands in 756, and the Papal States reached their greatest extent in 1649.</blockquote><a href="/government/theocracy">Theocracy · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
